1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,011
Last time, we scored the model's mistakes:

2
00:00:03,011 --> 00:00:04,848
the lower the score, the better.

3
00:00:05,198 --> 00:00:07,417
We scored every setting of the two knobs

4
00:00:07,417 --> 00:00:09,358
and drew the scores as a landscape.

5
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:11,411
The lowest point is the best setting.

6
00:00:11,761 --> 00:00:13,327
With two knobs, that's easy.

7
00:00:13,677 --> 00:00:15,293
But a big model can have

8
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:17,246
hundreds of billions of knobs

9
00:00:17,246 --> 00:00:18,593
way too many to map.

10
00:00:18,943 --> 00:00:20,696
So the model's like someone

11
00:00:20,696 --> 00:00:22,319
blindfolded on a mountain

12
00:00:22,319 --> 00:00:24,462
with no idea where the valley is.

13
00:00:24,812 --> 00:00:26,254
How does it find the bottom?

14
00:00:26,604 --> 00:00:29,172
It uses something called gradient descent.

15
00:00:29,172 --> 00:00:30,578
Let's see how it works.

16
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:40,877
So picture yourself up there

17
00:00:40,877 --> 00:00:42,784
trying to reach the valley.

18
00:00:43,134 --> 00:00:43,862
You can't see

19
00:00:43,862 --> 00:00:44,869
but your feet feel

20
00:00:44,869 --> 00:00:46,324
which way the ground tilts

21
00:00:46,324 --> 00:00:47,220
and how steeply.

22
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:49,851
So you feel for the steepest way down

23
00:00:49,851 --> 00:00:51,330
and take one small step.

24
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,691
Feel again, step again, and you get there.

25
00:00:55,041 --> 00:00:55,809
For the model

26
00:00:55,809 --> 00:00:58,230
the slope underfoot is how much the score

27
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:00,711
changes when you nudge each knob a little.

28
00:01:01,061 --> 00:01:02,807
Put those slopes together

29
00:01:02,807 --> 00:01:04,482
and that's the gradient.

30
00:01:04,482 --> 00:01:07,345
It shows every knob the fastest way down.

31
00:01:07,695 --> 00:01:09,332
Gradient descent turns all

32
00:01:09,332 --> 00:01:11,409
the knobs one small step that way

33
00:01:11,409 --> 00:01:13,171
then checks the slope again.

34
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:15,520
The learning rate sets the step size

35
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:17,685
and you pick it before training starts.

36
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,790
Let's try it on the taxi map:

37
00:01:23,790 --> 00:01:26,536
base fare across, per-kilometer rate up.

38
00:01:26,886 --> 00:01:28,770
Darker means a higher score.

39
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:30,251
The fares are made up.

40
00:01:30,601 --> 00:01:32,641
We start at a 5 dollar base

41
00:01:32,641 --> 00:01:34,982
fare and 2 dollars a kilometer:

42
00:01:34,982 --> 00:01:37,022
a score of 78, way up high.

43
00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:38,906
First, feel the slope:

44
00:01:38,906 --> 00:01:40,928
nudge each knob up ten cents.

45
00:01:41,278 --> 00:01:42,794
Raise the base fare

46
00:01:42,794 --> 00:01:44,709
and the score drops 1.7.

47
00:01:44,709 --> 00:01:46,943
Raise the per-kilometer rate

48
00:01:46,943 --> 00:01:48,379
and it drops 11.2.

49
00:01:48,729 --> 00:01:49,634
So turn both up

50
00:01:49,634 --> 00:01:50,901
but the per-kilometer

51
00:01:50,901 --> 00:01:52,228
slope is much steeper.

52
00:01:52,578 --> 00:01:54,930
Our first step turns that knob the most.

53
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,403
In three steps

54
00:01:56,403 --> 00:01:59,290
the score falls from 78 to 25, 12, 9

55
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:01,215
and we're in the valley.

56
00:02:01,565 --> 00:02:03,638
The floor is a long, gentle trench.

57
00:02:03,988 --> 00:02:05,573
The base fare creeps up

58
00:02:05,573 --> 00:02:07,847
the per-kilometer rate eases back

59
00:02:07,847 --> 00:02:09,088
and we inch along.

60
00:02:09,438 --> 00:02:11,656
Gentler slopes mean smaller steps

61
00:02:11,656 --> 00:02:12,731
so we slow down.

62
00:02:13,081 --> 00:02:15,334
After a thousand-odd steps

63
00:02:15,334 --> 00:02:17,067
the score's about 2.

64
00:02:17,067 --> 00:02:19,233
The true minimum is 1.97.

65
00:02:19,583 --> 00:02:21,844
The knobs end up near a ten-dollar

66
00:02:21,844 --> 00:02:24,238
base fare and two fifty a kilometer.

67
00:02:24,588 --> 00:02:26,470
It never saw the whole map.

68
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:28,841
It only felt the ground underfoot.

69
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:36,255
That run used a learning rate of 0.005.

70
00:02:36,255 --> 00:02:38,447
What if we'd picked wrong?

71
00:02:38,797 --> 00:02:41,383
Here are three paths from the same start

72
00:02:41,383 --> 00:02:42,740
same number of steps.

73
00:02:43,090 --> 00:02:44,648
Make it ten times smaller

74
00:02:44,648 --> 00:02:47,017
and it heads the right way but crawls.

75
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:49,115
It ends at a score of 5.5

76
00:02:49,115 --> 00:02:50,933
still far from the bottom.

77
00:02:51,283 --> 00:02:53,282
Make it about four times the original

78
00:02:53,282 --> 00:02:55,172
and it overshoots across the valley

79
00:02:55,172 --> 00:02:57,117
ending higher than where it started.

80
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:59,431
Then it climbs higher and higher

81
00:02:59,431 --> 00:03:00,351
past a hundred.

82
00:03:00,701 --> 00:03:02,739
Five steps in, it's off the map.

83
00:03:03,089 --> 00:03:04,720
Our original rate walks

84
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,351
steadily to the bottom.

85
00:03:06,701 --> 00:03:08,497
That's why the learning rate is often

86
00:03:08,497 --> 00:03:10,729
the single most important setting in training.

87
00:03:11,079 --> 00:03:13,696
Too big, and it bounces right off the map.

88
00:03:13,696 --> 00:03:15,565
Too small, and you waste time.

89
00:03:19,667 --> 00:03:22,204
Big models use gradient descent too

90
00:03:22,204 --> 00:03:23,508
with two upgrades.

91
00:03:23,858 --> 00:03:25,800
First, there's too much data

92
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,603
to read it all every step.

93
00:03:27,953 --> 00:03:30,306
So each step grabs a small random batch

94
00:03:30,306 --> 00:03:32,599
roughly estimates the slope, and goes.

95
00:03:32,599 --> 00:03:34,711
That's stochastic gradient descent.

96
00:03:35,061 --> 00:03:35,973
The path wobbles

97
00:03:35,973 --> 00:03:37,739
but it saves loads of computing

98
00:03:37,739 --> 00:03:39,676
and still ends up near the bottom.

99
00:03:40,026 --> 00:03:41,639
GPT-3, for example

100
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:43,790
saw only about 1/100,000

101
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:46,479
of its training data per step.

102
00:03:46,829 --> 00:03:48,653
Second, the steps get smarter.

103
00:03:48,653 --> 00:03:50,234
A method called Adam gives

104
00:03:50,234 --> 00:03:51,875
each knob its own step size

105
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:53,395
based on its past slopes.

106
00:03:53,745 --> 00:03:55,530
GPT-3 was trained with Adam

107
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:58,175
and Meta's Llama uses a tweaked version.

108
00:03:58,525 --> 00:04:00,547
That's how GPT-3's 175

109
00:04:00,547 --> 00:04:02,754
billion knobs got turned

110
00:04:02,754 --> 00:04:05,052
one small step at a time.

111
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:09,692
Gradient descent is walking downhill blindfolded:

112
00:04:09,692 --> 00:04:11,606
feel for the steepest way down

113
00:04:11,606 --> 00:04:13,009
and take a small step.

114
00:04:13,359 --> 00:04:14,983
And the learning rate sets

115
00:04:14,983 --> 00:04:16,357
the size of each step.

116
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:18,450
But a model with just two

117
00:04:18,450 --> 00:04:20,472
knobs can only learn so much.

118
00:04:20,822 --> 00:04:22,351
Next time, we'll stack lots

119
00:04:22,351 --> 00:04:23,766
of little units in layers

120
00:04:23,766 --> 00:04:25,068
each with its own knobs

121
00:04:25,068 --> 00:04:26,257
and see what happens.

122
00:04:26,607 --> 00:04:28,482
That's called a neural network.
